# Line endings are a correctness concern here, not a style preference.
#
# Source text is canonical UTF-8 with LF bytes on every platform, and test
# fixtures are compared byte for byte. A checkout that translated line endings
# would break those comparisons for a reason that has nothing to do with the
# code under test.
#
# Normalize everything to LF in the repository and on checkout.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Files whose exact bytes are the thing under test are marked binary outright,
# so no filter, no autocrlf setting, and no future .gitattributes edit can touch
# them. eol=lf above would be enough today; -text is enough forever.
#
# HTTP is the reason this matters concretely: request fixtures are
# CRLF-framed, and a fixture whose CRLFs became LFs would still parse as a
# plausible message while testing the wrong grammar.
test/fixtures/** -text
*.expected -text

# The vendored llhttp corpus is upstream's bytes, never edited, checksummed
# in test/vendor/llhttp/PROVENANCE and verified by a registered spec. It is
# marked binary for the same reason as the fixtures above, one step earlier:
# no fixture in it contains a CR byte *today*, so eol=lf would be harmless
# now -- but these are HTTP fixtures, upstream adds to them, and the first one
# carrying a literal CR would either break every checksum or, worse, parse as
# a plausible message while testing the wrong grammar. -text is enough forever.
test/vendor/** -text

# Project-internal notes, left out of release archives (`git archive`, and the
# source tarballs GitHub builds from a tag). The design records, lessons and
# agent instructions are for working on this repository, not for using it.
/.plans export-ignore
/AGENTS.md export-ignore
